    <main class="lesson-content">
        <article class="lesson-article">
            <header class="lesson-header">
                <span class="lesson-number">Lesson 4</span>
                <h1>Operators and Expressions</h1>
                <div class="lesson-meta">
                    <span class="lesson-duration">
                        <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                            <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                            <polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/>
                        </svg>
                        30 min
                    </span>
                    <span class="lesson-level beginner">Beginner</span>
                </div>
            </header>

            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Working with Operators</h2>
                    <p>Operators are symbols that tell the compiler to perform specific operations on values. In this lesson, you'll learn about the different types of operators in C# and how to combine them into expressions.</p>
                    
                    <p>By the end of this lesson, you'll be able to perform calculations, compare values, and combine conditions to make your programs more powerful.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Arithmetic Operators</h2>
                    <p>These operators perform mathematical calculations:</p>

                    <div class="operator-table">
                        <div class="operator-row header">
                            <span>Operator</span>
                            <span>Name</span>
                            <span>Example</span>
                            <span>Result</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>+</code>
                            <span>Addition</span>
                            <code>5 + 3</code>
                            <span>8</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>-</code>
                            <span>Subtraction</span>
                            <code>10 - 4</code>
                            <span>6</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>*</code>
                            <span>Multiplication</span>
                            <code>6 * 7</code>
                            <span>42</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>/</code>
                            <span>Division</span>
                            <code>20 / 4</code>
                            <span>5</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>%</code>
                            <span>Modulus (Remainder)</span>
                            <code>17 % 5</code>
                            <span>2</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">int a = 10;
int b = 3;

Console.WriteLine(a + b);   // 13
Console.WriteLine(a - b);   // 7
Console.WriteLine(a * b);   // 30
Console.WriteLine(a / b);   // 3 (integer division!)
Console.WriteLine(a % b);   // 1 (remainder)</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box warning">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <path d="M10.29 3.86L1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Watch Out: Integer Division</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>When dividing two integers, C# performs <strong>integer division</strong> and discards the decimal part. Use <code>double</code> or <code>decimal</code> if you need decimal results.</p>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Integer division vs floating-point division
int x = 7;
int y = 2;

Console.WriteLine(x / y);           // 3 (decimal discarded)
Console.WriteLine(x / (double)y);   // 3.5 (cast to double first)
Console.WriteLine(7.0 / 2);         // 3.5 (using double literals)</code></pre>

                    <h3>The Modulus Operator</h3>
                    <p>The modulus operator (<code>%</code>) returns the remainder after division. It's incredibly useful for:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Check if a number is even or odd
int number = 17;
bool isEven = number % 2 == 0;    // false (17 is odd)

// Cycle through values (0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2, ...)
int index = 0;
index = (index + 1) % 3;  // Cycles: 0 → 1 → 2 → 0 → 1 → ...

// Extract digits
int value = 12345;
int lastDigit = value % 10;       // 5
int lastTwoDigits = value % 100;  // 45</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Assignment Operators</h2>
                    <p>Assignment operators store values in variables:</p>

                    <div class="operator-table">
                        <div class="operator-row header">
                            <span>Operator</span>
                            <span>Example</span>
                            <span>Equivalent To</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>=</code>
                            <code>x = 5</code>
                            <span>Assign 5 to x</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>+=</code>
                            <code>x += 3</code>
                            <code>x = x + 3</code>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>-=</code>
                            <code>x -= 2</code>
                            <code>x = x - 2</code>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>*=</code>
                            <code>x *= 4</code>
                            <code>x = x * 4</code>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>/=</code>
                            <code>x /= 2</code>
                            <code>x = x / 2</code>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>%=</code>
                            <code>x %= 3</code>
                            <code>x = x % 3</code>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">int score = 100;

score += 50;   // score is now 150
score -= 25;   // score is now 125
score *= 2;    // score is now 250
score /= 5;    // score is now 50

Console.WriteLine(score);  // 50</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Increment and Decrement</h2>
                    <p>These operators increase or decrease a value by 1:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">int count = 5;

count++;    // count is now 6 (same as count += 1)
count--;    // count is now 5 (same as count -= 1)

// Prefix vs Postfix
int a = 5;
int b = 5;

Console.WriteLine(++a);   // 6 (increment THEN use)
Console.WriteLine(b++);   // 5 (use THEN increment)
Console.WriteLine(b);     // 6 (now it's incremented)</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Prefix vs Postfix</span>
                        </div>
                        <p><code>++x</code> (prefix) increments first, then returns the value. <code>x++</code> (postfix) returns the value first, then increments. When used alone on a line, they work the same way.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Comparison Operators</h2>
                    <p>Comparison operators compare two values and return a <code>bool</code> (true or false):</p>

                    <div class="operator-table">
                        <div class="operator-row header">
                            <span>Operator</span>
                            <span>Meaning</span>
                            <span>Example</span>
                            <span>Result</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>==</code>
                            <span>Equal to</span>
                            <code>5 == 5</code>
                            <span>true</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>!=</code>
                            <span>Not equal to</span>
                            <code>5 != 3</code>
                            <span>true</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>&gt;</code>
                            <span>Greater than</span>
                            <code>10 &gt; 5</code>
                            <span>true</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>&lt;</code>
                            <span>Less than</span>
                            <code>3 &lt; 8</code>
                            <span>true</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>&gt;=</code>
                            <span>Greater than or equal</span>
                            <code>5 &gt;= 5</code>
                            <span>true</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>&lt;=</code>
                            <span>Less than or equal</span>
                            <code>4 &lt;= 3</code>
                            <span>false</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">int age = 25;
int minAge = 18;
int maxAge = 65;

bool isAdult = age &gt;= minAge;           // true
bool isRetired = age &gt;= maxAge;         // false
bool isWorkingAge = age &gt;= 18 &amp;&amp; age &lt; 65;  // true

// Comparing strings
string name = "Alice";
bool isAlice = name == "Alice";         // true
bool isNotBob = name != "Bob";          // true</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box warning">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <path d="M10.29 3.86L1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Common Mistake</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>Don't confuse <code>=</code> (assignment) with <code>==</code> (comparison). <code>x = 5</code> assigns 5 to x, while <code>x == 5</code> checks if x equals 5.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Logical Operators</h2>
                    <p>Logical operators combine boolean expressions:</p>

                    <div class="operator-table">
                        <div class="operator-row header">
                            <span>Operator</span>
                            <span>Name</span>
                            <span>Description</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>&&</code>
                            <span>AND</span>
                            <span>True if BOTH conditions are true</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>||</code>
                            <span>OR</span>
                            <span>True if AT LEAST ONE condition is true</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="operator-row">
                            <code>!</code>
                            <span>NOT</span>
                            <span>Inverts the boolean value</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">bool hasLicense = true;
bool hasInsurance = true;
bool isSuspended = false;

// AND - both must be true
bool canDrive = hasLicense && hasInsurance;  // true

// OR - at least one must be true
bool hasDocuments = hasLicense || hasInsurance;  // true

// NOT - inverts the value
bool isActive = !isSuspended;  // true

// Combining operators
bool canLegallyDrive = hasLicense && hasInsurance && !isSuspended;  // true</code></pre>

                    <h3>Truth Tables</h3>
                    <div class="truth-tables">
                        <div class="truth-table">
                            <h4>AND (&&)</h4>
                            <div class="table-grid">
                                <span>A</span><span>B</span><span>A && B</span>
                                <span>true</span><span>true</span><span class="result-true">true</span>
                                <span>true</span><span>false</span><span class="result-false">false</span>
                                <span>false</span><span>true</span><span class="result-false">false</span>
                                <span>false</span><span>false</span><span class="result-false">false</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="truth-table">
                            <h4>OR (||)</h4>
                            <div class="table-grid">
                                <span>A</span><span>B</span><span>A || B</span>
                                <span>true</span><span>true</span><span class="result-true">true</span>
                                <span>true</span><span>false</span><span class="result-true">true</span>
                                <span>false</span><span>true</span><span class="result-true">true</span>
                                <span>false</span><span>false</span><span class="result-false">false</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Short-Circuit Evaluation</h3>
                    <p>C# uses <strong>short-circuit evaluation</strong> – it stops evaluating as soon as the result is known:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// With &&, if the first condition is false, the second isn't checked
bool result = false && SomeExpensiveMethod();  // Method never called!

// With ||, if the first condition is true, the second isn't checked
bool result = true || SomeExpensiveMethod();   // Method never called!

// Useful for null checks
string name = null;
bool isValid = name != null && name.Length > 0;  // Safe! Length not checked if null</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>String Operators</h2>
                    <p>The <code>+</code> operator concatenates (joins) strings:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">string firstName = "John";
string lastName = "Doe";

// Concatenation with +
string fullName = firstName + " " + lastName;  // "John Doe"

// Better: String interpolation
string greeting = $"Hello, {firstName} {lastName}!";  // "Hello, John Doe!"

// Concatenating with numbers (auto-converts to string)
int age = 30;
string message = firstName + " is " + age + " years old.";
// Better:
string message2 = $"{firstName} is {age} years old.";</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box success">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Best Practice</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>Prefer string interpolation (<code>$"..."</code>) over concatenation with <code>+</code>. It's more readable and often more efficient.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Null Operators</h2>
                    <p>C# provides special operators for handling null values:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">string? name = null;

// Null-coalescing operator (??) - provide a default
string displayName = name ?? "Guest";  // "Guest"

// Null-coalescing assignment (??=) - assign only if null
name ??= "Default";  // name is now "Default"

// Null-conditional operator (?.) - safe member access
int? length = name?.Length;  // null if name is null, otherwise the length

// Combining null operators
string upper = name?.ToUpper() ?? "N/A";</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Ternary Operator</h2>
                    <p>The ternary operator is a shortcut for simple if-else statements:</p>

                    <div class="syntax-box">
                        <code>condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse</code>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">int age = 20;

// Traditional if-else
string status;
if (age >= 18)
{
    status = "Adult";
}
else
{
    status = "Minor";
}

// Same thing with ternary operator
string status = age >= 18 ? "Adult" : "Minor";

// More examples
int score = 85;
string grade = score >= 90 ? "A" : score >= 80 ? "B" : score >= 70 ? "C" : "F";

// Use for simple conditions - don't over-nest!
int max = a > b ? a : b;
string display = name ?? "Anonymous";</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Readability Tip</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>Use the ternary operator for simple conditions. For complex logic, use regular if-else statements – readability is more important than brevity.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Operator Precedence</h2>
                    <p>When multiple operators are used, C# follows a specific order of operations:</p>

                    <div class="precedence-list">
                        <div class="precedence-item">
                            <span class="precedence-rank">1</span>
                            <span>Parentheses <code>()</code></span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="precedence-item">
                            <span class="precedence-rank">2</span>
                            <span>Unary <code>!</code> <code>++</code> <code>--</code></span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="precedence-item">
                            <span class="precedence-rank">3</span>
                            <span>Multiplication/Division <code>*</code> <code>/</code> <code>%</code></span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="precedence-item">
                            <span class="precedence-rank">4</span>
                            <span>Addition/Subtraction <code>+</code> <code>-</code></span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="precedence-item">
                            <span class="precedence-rank">5</span>
                            <span>Comparison <code>&lt;</code> <code>&gt;</code> <code>&lt;=</code> <code>&gt;=</code></span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="precedence-item">
                            <span class="precedence-rank">6</span>
                            <span>Equality <code>==</code> <code>!=</code></span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="precedence-item">
                            <span class="precedence-rank">7</span>
                            <span>Logical AND <code>&&</code></span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="precedence-item">
                            <span class="precedence-rank">8</span>
                            <span>Logical OR <code>||</code></span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="precedence-item">
                            <span class="precedence-rank">9</span>
                            <span>Ternary <code>?:</code></span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="precedence-item">
                            <span class="precedence-rank">10</span>
                            <span>Assignment <code>=</code> <code>+=</code> etc.</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Precedence examples
int result = 2 + 3 * 4;        // 14 (not 20!) - multiplication first
int result2 = (2 + 3) * 4;     // 20 - parentheses override

bool check = 5 &gt; 3 &amp;&amp; 2 &lt; 4;   // true - comparisons, then AND
bool check2 = true || false &amp;&amp; false;  // true - AND before OR

// When in doubt, use parentheses for clarity!
bool clear = (5 &gt; 3) &amp;&amp; (2 &lt; 4);  // Same as above, but clearer</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Arithmetic operators (<code>+ - * / %</code>) perform mathematical calculations.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Comparison operators (<code>== != &lt; &gt;</code>) return boolean results.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Logical operators (<code>&& || !</code>) combine boolean expressions.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>The ternary operator (<code>?:</code>) is a shortcut for simple if-else.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use parentheses to make operator precedence clear.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Practice Exercises</h2>
                    
                    <div class="exercise-box">
                        <h4>Exercise 4.1: Calculator</h4>
                        <p>Create a program that asks the user for two numbers and displays the result of all five arithmetic operations (+, -, *, /, %).</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exercise-box">
                        <h4>Exercise 4.2: Age Checker</h4>
                        <p>Create a program that asks for a user's age and determines:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Are they old enough to vote? (18+)</li>
                            <li>Are they old enough to drink? (21+)</li>
                            <li>Are they a teenager? (13-19)</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Use comparison and logical operators to check all conditions.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exercise-box">
                        <h4>Exercise 4.3: Grade Calculator</h4>
                        <p>Create a program that takes a numeric score (0-100) and displays the letter grade using the ternary operator:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>90-100: A</li>
                            <li>80-89: B</li>
                            <li>70-79: C</li>
                            <li>60-69: D</li>
                            <li>Below 60: F</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exercise-box">
                        <h4>Exercise 4.4: Even/Odd Checker</h4>
                        <p>Create a program that asks for a number and tells the user if it's even or odd. Use the modulus operator (<code>%</code>).</p>
                        <p><strong>Bonus:</strong> Also check if the number is divisible by 5.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>
            </div>

            <LessonFooter 
                PreviousUrl="/training/csharp-fundamentals/lesson/3" 
                PreviousText="Previous Lesson"
                NextUrl="/training/csharp-fundamentals/lesson/5" 
                NextText="Next Lesson" />
        </article>
    </main>
</div>

